<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Basra</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Basra is a port city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, founded around 636 as a garrison town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>. It became a major commercial and intellectual centre of the early Islamic world. In the 1770s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a>'s forces besieged and captured it, one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand Empire</a>'s main military ventures.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/basra.jpg" alt="Basra" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: File:Basra city.jpg · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basra_city.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Basra lies on the banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shatt-al-arab">Shatt al-Arab</a>, which empties into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, near the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Its Arabic name, al-Basrah, means the overwatcher, perhaps an allusion to its origin as an Arab military base; others derive it from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> basratha, a place of huts or settlement. It was built in 636 as a camp for the Arab tribesmen of the armies of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun</a> caliph Umar. The original site, about 15 km south-west of the modern city, is marked by the Imam Ali Mosque, and Basra is home to the first mosque built outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>, who took Basra in the late 740s, it became an intellectual centre and home to the Basra School of Grammar, rival and sister of the school of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufa">Kufa</a>. Its notable figures include the polymath <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-haytham">Ibn al-Haytham</a>, the writer al-Jahiz and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> mystic Rabia Basri. The city was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanj">Zanj</a> rebellion of agricultural slaves, whose forces sacked it in 871. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qarmatians">Qarmatians</a> invaded and devastated it in 923.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> under Hulegu Khan sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> in 1258, Basra by some accounts capitulated to avoid a massacre. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> visited in the 14th century. Portugal controlled the city from 1526, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> captured it in 1668. It was under Safavid control from 1697 to 1701, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zand-dynasty">Zand dynasty</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karim-khan-zand">Karim Khan Zand</a> occupied it briefly after a long siege. In 1884 the Ottomans created the vilayet of Basra. British forces captured the city in 1914, and it passed to Mandatory Iraq and then to the Kingdom of Iraq, independent in 1932.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Basra is the capital of Basra Governorate and the third largest city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a>, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. Its position on the Gulf makes it a major port, and it is the country's only coastal region, along with its adjoining governorate. During <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> it was an important port through which much equipment sent to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> flowed. The University of Basrah was founded in 1964. The population is mostly Arab Shia Muslims, with a large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> minority.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Basra” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>This system kept conquered agriculture productive and channelled its surplus into stipends that sustained the Arab military establishment in purpose-built garrison cities, Kufa and <b>Basra</b> in Iraq and Fustat in Egypt, which were at once military camps, administrative centres and growing commercial hubs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1776 his forces captured <b>Basra</b> from the Ottomans after a long siege.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zand-empire">Zand Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…1290 the Dominican Riccoldo da Monte di Croce met them in Mesopotamia, and in the 16th and 17th centuries members of the Discalced Carmelite mission in <b>Basra</b> called them “Christians of Saint John”, a name that has been judged a misnomer, since Mandaeans consider Jesus a false prophet.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mandaeism">Mandaeism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Ships from <b>Basra</b> and Siraf sailed to India and China, and a whole section of the market of east Baghdad sold Chinese goods, from silk to porcelain; caravans brought spices, gold and slaves, and Jewish merchants known as the Radhanites linked the Muslim world with Christian Europe.</blockquote><a href="/societies/abbasid-caliphate">Social Hierarchy of the Abbasid Caliphate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qarmatians, an Ismaili movement based in eastern Arabia, captured and looted the Abbasid city of <b>Basra</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Near al-Mada'in in central Iraq, the armies of the Hamdanids of Mosul and the Baridis of <b>Basra</b> began a four-day battle for Baghdad, the Abbasid capital.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-16">16 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
